  <h2>What Release Readiness Actually Means (It's More Than Passing Regression)</h2>
  <p>Many teams define "release ready" as "regression suite passed." That is necessary, but it is not sufficient. A regression suite validates that the features you tested in the way you expected to test them produce the results you expected. It does not validate that the deployment process itself is safe, that the rollback path works, that monitoring is configured, or that the support team knows what changed.</p>
  <p>A complete release readiness framework covers all of this:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Coverage check:</strong> What percentage of the defined test scope was executed? What remains untested, and why? Is the untested scope low enough risk to accept?</li>
    <li><strong>Critical path validation:</strong> The most important user flows – the ones where a bug causes direct business impact – have been verified manually and/or with smoke tests, regardless of what the regression suite says.</li>
    <li><strong>Known issues list:</strong> Every open bug in the current release scope is reviewed. Blockers are resolved. Non-blockers are documented with a rationale for why they are acceptable to ship. This list exists so decisions are explicit, not implicit.</li>
    <li><strong>Rollback plan:</strong> What do you do if the release causes an incident in the first hour after deployment? The rollback plan is documented and has been tested – not just written.</li>
    <li><strong>Monitoring and alerting:</strong> The monitoring that will detect a problem with the new release is configured and tested before the release happens. Not configured after the incident that reveals the gap.</li>
    <li><strong>Communication plan:</strong> Who gets notified when the release goes out? Are there customer-facing changes that require documentation or support team briefing?</li>
  </ul>

