  <div class="faq-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">What is a release readiness assessment?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">A release readiness assessment is a structured evaluation of whether a software release is ready to deploy to production. It covers test coverage completion, known issue review, rollback plan validation, monitoring setup, and stakeholder sign-off – producing a clear go/no-go decision with documented justification.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">What should be in a release checklist?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">Regression test suite completion and results, critical path smoke test results, known issues list with documented acceptance decisions, database migration validation (if applicable), rollback plan confirmation, monitoring and alerting verification, and stakeholder sign-offs.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">How do you know a release is truly ready?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">A release is ready when all items on the release checklist are completed, all blocking defects are resolved, all deferred defects are documented with stakeholder acceptance, the rollback plan has been tested, and the monitoring that will confirm successful deployment is in place.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">What is a smoke test suite?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">A smoke test suite is a focused set of 15-30 tests that validates the most critical paths in your application. It should run in under 5 minutes and confirm that the application starts up and basic functionality works before investing time in full regression. Runs immediately after every deployment to staging and production.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">How do you handle hotfixes and emergency releases?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">Hotfixes require a compressed release process, not no process. A hotfix release checklist covers: the specific fix has been tested, the fix has not introduced regressions in the areas it touches, a rollback plan exists, and someone with release authority has signed off.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

